  "narrative": "During the 1980s, Black and African women's hairstyles reflected a rich cultural identity and resilience in the face of societal norms. The Jheri curl, invented by Jheri Redding, was a popular permanent wave hairstyle that gave wearers a glossy, loosely curled look, touted as a \"wash and wear\" style that was easier to care for than relaxers. This era also saw the continued popularity of traditional hairstyles such as cornrows, which were considered a staple in many African cultures, as well as box braids, a protective style that added thickness and versatility to natural hair. While documentation from this era is limited, it's clear that Black and African women's hair culture was characterized by a strong sense of identity and self-expression, with hairstyles serving as a means of resistance against the dominant beauty standards of the time.",
